# One flavor

This file is named `syntax.sx`. Every other page in this project is a `.md`
file, and that is the only difference between them — rename this one to
`syntax.md` and not one byte of the output changes.

## Why there are no dialects

strike has no flavor switch, no dialect flag, and no branch in the renderer that
asks which extension it is looking at. There is one parser and one pipeline:

. plain markdown is a **subset** of strikedown, so every `.md` file is already a
  valid strikedown document
. superset syntax is **additive**, so it works in a `.md` file too — nothing
  gates it behind the extension

/skinny(80%)

/center()

The extension is a hint to your editor, not an instruction to the renderer.

## So what is it for?

Two reasons to reach for `.sx`:

// why grid(2)

**Signalling intent**

A `.sx` extension tells a reader — human or tool — that this document leans on
the superset and will look wrong in a plain markdown viewer.

// --

**Keeping `.md` honest**

If a folder is going to be read on GitHub as well as through strike, keeping the
superset in `.sx` files makes the split explicit.

// end why

> [!NOTE]
> Neither reason is enforced. A `.md` file full of groups and commands renders
> exactly the same as this one, and a `.sx` file containing nothing but plain
> markdown is a perfectly ordinary document.

## The same features, in a .sx file

Here is superset syntax doing superset things, in a file with the other
extension — proof by construction.

// proof grid(2) color(muted)

`grid(2)` on a group whose opener also carries `color(muted)`: two commands, one
directive line.

// --

The [layout page](layout.md) explains both, and this is a cross-document link
resolving from a `.sx` file to a `.md` one — which also needs no special case.

// end proof

/indent(2)

Math works too: $\nabla \cdot \mathbf{E} = \rho / \varepsilon_0$.

  And a whitespace-indented paragraph is still one step of indent, because the
  block loop that reads it never learned what file it was reading.
